const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/freezer-repair/frigidaire-not-freezing/";

const title = "Frigidaire Freezer Not Freezing? Causes & Fix";
const description = "Frigidaire freezer not staying frozen? Usually dirty coils, the defrost system, or the control board. Safe checks, when to call. Same-day in Los Angeles, $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Frigidaire freezer not freezing?",
    a: "On a Frigidaire freezer that runs but won't stay frozen, the usual causes are dirty condenser coils, a defrost system iced over the evaporator, a failed evaporator fan, a bad start relay, the control board, or a sealed-system/compressor fault. A freezer should hold about 0°F; when it drifts warmer and food softens, one of those is why. Frigidaire builds a lot of standalone chest and upright deep-freezers, and two Frigidaire-specific things matter: the electronic control board is a known weak point on the frost-free models, and a garage-kept freezer can be fooled by a cold ambient temperature. Cleaning coils and checking the door seal are homeowner-safe; the defrost parts, fan, board, and sealed system are a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "My Frigidaire freezer is in the garage — could that be why it won't freeze?",
    a: "Possibly, and it's worth ruling out before assuming a fault. A standard freezer's thermostat senses the freezer's internal temperature, but the compressor's behavior is also affected by the surrounding air. In a very cold garage (an unusually cold LA night, or a cold-storage space), some freezers can under-run the compressor and let the interior drift up — and in a very hot garage in summer, the compressor struggles to shed heat and can't keep up. If yours isn't a garage-ready model, extreme ambient temperatures can genuinely affect freezing. Frigidaire sells 'garage-ready' units built for this; if yours isn't one and it's in an unconditioned garage, that may be part of the story alongside anything mechanical."
  },
  {
    q: "My Frigidaire is a chest freezer with frost buildup — is it broken?",
    a: "Probably not — most chest freezers are manual-defrost, and frost buildup on the walls is normal for them, not a fault. A manual-defrost chest freezer is designed to accumulate frost over time, and you're meant to empty it and defrost it periodically (every so many months, or when the frost gets thick). Heavy frost can eventually insulate the walls and hurt performance, which reads as 'not freezing as well.' So if it's a manual-defrost chest unit, try a full manual defrost first. If it won't hold temperature even after defrosting, or it's a frost-free upright building frost it shouldn't, then it's a real fault — often the defrost system or the control board."
  },
  {
    q: "Is the control board a common Frigidaire freezer problem?",
    a: "Yes — on frost-free Frigidaire units the electronic control board is a documented weak point, and it can cause not-freezing in ways that mimic other parts. A failing board can stop running the defrost cycle (so frost builds and blocks airflow), misread the temperature, or not cycle the compressor correctly, so the symptoms look like a defrost, sensor, or compressor problem when the board is the root cause. That's exactly why we test the board rather than swap parts by guess — replacing a fan or a sensor won't fix a freezer whose board isn't commanding the cooling correctly. It's the same control-board pattern we cover on our Frigidaire refrigerator not cooling page."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Frigidaire freezer that won't freeze myself?",
    a: "Some first steps, yes. Vacuum the condenser coils, make sure the door or lid seals fully, confirm the temperature setting and that interior vents aren't blocked, and — on a manual-defrost chest freezer — do a full defrost if frost has built up thick. If it's in a garage, consider whether extreme ambient temperature is a factor. Those handle a fair share of cases. What to leave to a tech: the defrost heater/sensor/control, the evaporator or condenser fan, the start relay, the control board, and anything in the sealed system or compressor. Those carry shock and refrigerant risks. If it stays warm after a coil clean and a good seal, it's a service call."
  }
];

