<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="freezer-repair/frigidaire-not-freezing"
  eyebrow="Frigidaire Freezer Not Freezing · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Frigidaire Freezer Not Freezing? Coils, Defrost, or Board"
  subtitle="A Frigidaire that won't stay frozen is usually dirty coils, the defrost system, or the control board — and on a chest freezer or a garage unit, two things fool people first. Here's what to check."
  heroImageTitle="Frigidaire Freezer Not Freezing Diagnosis and Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-freeze Frigidaire is coils, defrost, the board — or a false alarm.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Frigidaire freezer runs but won't stay frozen, the causes are dirty condenser coils, a defrost system iced over, a failed evaporator fan, a bad start relay, the control board, or a sealed-system/compressor fault. A freezer should hold about 0°F. Two Frigidaire-specific things trip people up first: on frost-free models the electronic control board is a known weak point that can mimic other faults, and on the standalone chest and upright deep-freezers Frigidaire is known for, a cold garage or (on a manual-defrost chest) normal frost buildup can look like a failure when nothing's broken. So rule out the easy stuff — coils, seal, ambient temperature, a manual defrost — then it's the mechanical causes. Coils and seal are homeowner-safe; defrost, fan, board, and sealed system are a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Keep the door shut while you wait.</strong> Per USDA guidance, a full freezer holds a safe temperature about 48 hours (24 hours if half-full) if the door stays closed — so a not-freezing Frigidaire is worth a same-day call before food is at risk.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 FRIGIDAIRE-SPECIFIC -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Two Frigidaire things to rule out first</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The control board, and the standalone-freezer false alarms.</h2>

    <p><strong>The control board.</strong> On frost-free Frigidaire units the electronic control board is a documented weak point, and a failing board causes not-freezing in ways that mimic other parts — it can stop running the defrost cycle (frost builds, airflow blocks), misread the temperature, or not cycle the compressor right. That's why we test the board rather than guess: swapping a fan or sensor won't fix a freezer whose board isn't commanding the cooling. It's the same pattern we cover on our <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/frigidaire-not-cooling/">Frigidaire refrigerator not cooling</a> page.</p>

    <p><strong>The standalone false alarms.</strong> Frigidaire is big in chest and upright deep-freezers, and two of those look like faults but aren't: a <em>manual-defrost chest freezer</em> is supposed to accumulate frost — defrost it fully before assuming it's broken — and a freezer in an unconditioned <em>garage</em> can be affected by extreme ambient temperature if it isn't a garage-ready model. Rule both out before paying for a repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Frigidaire freezer won't freeze</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust can't shed heat, so the freezer slowly loses its ability to hold 0°F. The most common homeowner-fixable cause, and worse in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Control board (frost-free models)</h3>
      <p>A documented Frigidaire weak point. A failing board can stop defrost, misread temperature, or mis-cycle the compressor — mimicking several faults. We test it rather than guess.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass. A thick, returning frost sheet on the back panel is the tell. Tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan</h3>
      <p>Circulates cold air over the coil. If it fails or frost jams it, the freezer warms though the compressor runs. Tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Start relay / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor won't start or runs without cooling, the relay or the sealed system is the fault — the higher-ticket end. A gauges-and-tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual not-freezing cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door or lid seal</strong> closes fully and nothing's holding it ajar.</li>
      <li><strong>On a manual-defrost chest freezer, do a full defrost</strong> if frost has built up thick — that's normal maintenance, not a repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Consider the location</strong> — if it's in a hot or very cold garage and isn't a garage-ready model, ambient temperature may be a factor.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, seal, a manual defrost, and checking the location are safe. The control board, defrost parts, evaporator/condenser fan, start relay, and sealed system carry shock and refrigerant risks — our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 SIBLING -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Chest, upright, or the freezer side of a fridge</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same diagnosis, whichever Frigidaire you have.</h2>

    <p>The causes are the same whether it's a Frigidaire chest freezer, an upright, or the freezer compartment of a Frigidaire refrigerator — coils, defrost, fan, board, and compressor work the same way (a manual-defrost chest just adds the defrost-it-first step). The same not-freezing fault on another brand: <a href="/services/freezer-repair/lg-not-freezing/">LG freezer not freezing</a>, or the general <a href="/services/freezer-repair/not-freezing/">freezer not freezing</a> guide. If it's your fridge and the freezer's fine but the fresh-food side is warm, see <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, defrosted, still not freezing?</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the seal's good, a manual-defrost unit has been defrosted, and location isn't the issue but the Frigidaire still won't hold 0°F, the fault is inside — the control board, the defrost system, the evaporator fan, the start relay, or the sealed system. Because the board is a known Frigidaire weak point, our techs test it rather than swap parts by guess, and carry the common Frigidaire defrost parts and fan motors for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/freezer-repair/not-freezing/">freezer not freezing</a> guide, <a href="/services/freezer-repair/ice-buildup/">freezer ice buildup</a> if it's mostly frost, the Frigidaire board pattern on <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/frigidaire-not-cooling/">Frigidaire refrigerator not cooling</a>, and the sibling <a href="/services/freezer-repair/lg-not-freezing/">LG not freezing</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/freezer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/freezer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/freezer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Frigidaire freezer not freezing: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Frigidaire freezer not freezing? Call today — food is on a clock.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

